{"kind":"artifact","text":"README.md at exact clean pushed head ee95fb23069e793c6a60415634fe851f7a30612d on request/readme-positioning. Successor to ba323b1f, answering codex's changes-requested verdict 0cab934c which I ratified at e6036d32. One blocker, and it was the sharpest of the three rounds.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. The text said a local resident may hold actor keys and sign for \"authenticated sessions\". That implies the resident authenticates a joining process as the actor it names. It does not, and I verified that rather than accepting the finding. internal/service/server.go handleAnnounce decodes an actor name from the request body, calls workspace.Actor on that name, and receives that actor's private key. There is no challenge and no proof that the caller is the named actor. The credential minted afterwards authenticates later use of an already-bound session; it says nothing about who opened it.\n\ndocs/reference/gs/serve.md states the boundary without flinching, and the README should not have been softer than our own reference page: the service holds their signing keys and signs on behalf of whichever trusted process asks, and every process inside this resident boundary can act as every actor key this application can open. It adds that loopback binding limits who can reach the service, is not a shared-host authentication system, and does not separate the actors from one another inside the boundary.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS NOW. A local resident may hold actor keys and sign on behalf of any trusted local process that asks, so a signature identifies the actor, not the process that requested it. That is the same fact the reference page states, in the paragraph whose entire purpose is attribution.\n\nAlso reflowed the source line break the previous edit left behind, which codex asked for in the same verdict.\n\nTHE PATTERN THIS COMPLETES, and I think it is the most useful thing to record here. Three review rounds on one page. In each round the surviving error was a claim about a trust boundary, and in each round the claim was more reassuring than the truth. First, that anyone with a clone can verify the whole history offline, when an ordinary clone does not fetch the sequence. Then, that every actor signs with its own key, when a resident may hold those keys. Now, that sessions are authenticated, when the resident signs for whichever local process asks. Everything else in the page got safer on the first pass. Only the security claims stayed optimistic, and each needed someone to open the enforcement site to find it out.\n\nI do not think this is carelessness about security specifically. It is that a positioning page rewards confident sentences, and the sentences that describe guarantees are exactly the ones where confidence is a claim rather than a style. A reviewer who reads for plausibility cannot catch these; only one who reads the code can. Codex read the code all three times.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal: gofmt silent, go build exit 0, go vet exit 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and no failures, docset inside that run.\n\nConfirmed unchanged and carried forward from the previous head, which codex verified independently: the four earlier factual corrections all hold, blog and video links resolve, no competitor is named anywhere, and the repeated first-application sentence is gone.\n\nThis still does not merge on a reviewer's approval alone. Hugh directed the positioning and ruled on the number; he has not seen the wording.","body":{"commit":"ee95fb23069e793c6a60415634fe851f7a30612d","path":"README.md"}}